WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Speaker 1: Welcome, everybody.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:06.319
So I'm going to talk today about the Biden
Putin summit that took place yesterday in

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:07.340
Geneva, Switzerland.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.820
We're going to start with Vladimir Putin's
press conference momentarily.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:15.599
Later, I want to talk about Donald Trump's
analysis, if you can use that term, which

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:17.130
you really shouldn't.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:23.699
Trump's analysis on Fox News of the summit,
and I hate that I even have to do this, but

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:28.840
I want to just remind everybody I don't want
war with Russia.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:34.540
The United States also does bad things, et
cetera.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:41.430
And every time I talk about this and I just
point out the way I see Vladimir Putin and

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:46.330
Russian government, I get all sorts of emails
from people saying, David, you're falling

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:47.980
for this and you're falling for that.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:52.030
And you should have this other opinion like
this person or that I'm not those people.

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:57.870
So if you want only to listen to someone with
that opinion, go to a different show.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:58.960
This is my view.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.530
And my view is not that I want war with Russia.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:08.790
My view is not that Putin is uniquely bad,
but there are some really interesting and

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:13.770
unique characteristics of how Vladimir Putin
handles handles himself with media and how

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:17.240
he runs his government that I think are worthy
of some discussion.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.510
So yesterday, Joe Biden and Vladimir Putin
had a meeting.

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.620
It was more than three hours long.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:27.300
The American right wing was hoping that Joe
Biden would embarrass himself and be unable

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.300
to speak.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:32.320
He, you know, be in a fit of acute dementia,
pooping his pants or whatever.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.740
That didn't happen.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:40.420
At the same time, there were some on the left
that often play coy with Putin who were hoping

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:46.670
that Biden would be exposed in this summit
as some kind of neocon war hawk who would

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.770
threaten Putin and go completely crazy.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.930
And that also didn't happen.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:57.870
And it was mostly unremarkable in that it
was normal diplomacy with a normal president

00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:59.790
and a normal secretary of state.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:07.810
The four years of Trump, where everything
is power games and often just placating authoritarian

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:13.520
leaders around the world, people forgot sort
of how diplomacy works and it's kind of boring.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.970
Biden goes in with the list of priorities
that he wants to mention and then the secretary

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.300
of state and foreign minister, whatever the
equivalent position is called in Russia, they

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.950
have sort of second tier things on their list
that they talk about.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:31.530
And it's a long and in some colloquial sense,
boring process of diplomacy.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:36.170
But the most interesting part for me was actually
the press conference that happened after the

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:37.200
event.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.890
Vladimir Putin first giving his press conference,
later Joe Biden doing his own press conference

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.020
and both taking questions from the media.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:48.819
I want to focus in on the Putin press conference
today because Vladimir Putin was actually

00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:52.240
very strongly confronted by reporters.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:58.739
But Putin is so good at just slipping around,
answering any questions that he ends up saying

00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:00.400
really nothing.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:05.090
Even when asked directly about the deaths
of his political opponents, the fact that

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.349
dissidents end up poisoned in Russia.

00:03:07.349 --> 00:03:09.150
So let's dove right in here.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:13.930
He's asked if he'll stop the cyber attacks
and stop the crackdowns on his opposition.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:15.560
See if you can find an answer here.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.850
Speaker 2: Thank you very much for giving
me this question.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:24.440
First of all, could you characterize the dynamic
between yourself and President Biden?

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.510
Was it hostile or was it friendly?

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:32.879
And secondly, throughout these conversations,
did you commit to ceasing carrying out cyber

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:34.180
attacks on the United States?

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.849
Did you commit to stopping, threatening Ukraine's
security?

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:44.630
And did you commit to stop cracking down on
the opposition in Russia?

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:54.260
Speaker 3: It should gear up shirts in the
first overall evaluation as far as that is

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.459
concerned, I don't think there was any kind
of hostility.

00:03:56.459 --> 00:03:57.739
On the contrary.

00:03:57.739 --> 00:04:04.440
Our meeting was obviously, you know, a fundamental
one.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.480
Many of our joint positions are divergent.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:14.730
But nevertheless, I think that both sides
manifested a determination to try and understand

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:18.349
each other and try and converge our positions.

00:04:18.349 --> 00:04:25.849
And I think it was very constructive as far
as cyber security is concerned.

00:04:25.849 --> 00:04:32.379
We agreed that we would begin consultations
on that issue.

00:04:32.379 --> 00:04:33.539
And I believe that's extraordinary.

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:36.580
Speaker 1: Just no answer.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:40.930
And and Putin is really amazing at this, quite
frankly.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:46.159
Asked about the treatment of dissident Alexei
Navalny, Putin does two things that he often

00:04:46.159 --> 00:04:47.159
does.

00:04:47.159 --> 00:04:48.699
These are classics from the Putin playbook.

00:04:48.699 --> 00:04:52.219
One, Putin won't say the guy's name.

00:04:52.219 --> 00:04:55.939
He'll refer to him as the person you mentioned
or the gentleman in question.

00:04:55.939 --> 00:04:58.370
And he says simply, well, the guy was breaking
the law

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:08.349
Speaker 3: as far as our non systematic or
nonstandard opposition is concerned.

00:05:08.349 --> 00:05:16.550
And the gentleman you quoted, first of all,
this man knew that he was breaking the law

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:17.969
of Russia.

00:05:17.969 --> 00:05:20.219
The US was doing OK.

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:29.210
He is somebody who has been twice convicted
even that, and he consciously ignored the

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:30.939
requirements of the law.

00:05:30.939 --> 00:05:35.050
The gentleman in question went abroad for
treatment.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:41.490
His registration was not asked for
Speaker 1: the gentleman you referenced.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:43.949
This is a classic Putin move.

00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:49.650
And of course, being suspected of breaking
the law, even if true, doesn't justify in

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:50.650
many countries.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:54.300
It doesn't justify or lead to the treatment
that Alexei Navalny has received.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.509
Now, guys, this doesn't mean the US doesn't
do bad things.

00:05:57.509 --> 00:06:01.389
This doesn't mean the U.S. doesn't have inhumane
jails and prisons.

00:06:01.389 --> 00:06:07.210
But it's just our analysis of what it is that
is happening in Russia and how it is that

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.430
Putin responds to questions about it.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:15.710
An ABC reporter is called upon for a question
and says to Putin, the list of your political

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:19.360
opponents who are dead, imprisoned or jailed
is long.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:20.539
What are you afraid of?

00:06:20.539 --> 00:06:25.560
And Putin either doesn't understand the question
or more likely, Putin's a very smart guy.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.560
Putin pretends not to understand and successfully
avoids actually answering.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:34.300
Speaker 3: Yes, Americans, BBC News, our American
colleagues.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:41.539
Speaker 4: Mr. President, thank you so much
for taking my question, President Biden has

00:06:41.539 --> 00:06:46.240
said that he would respond if cyber attacks
from Russia do not stop.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:48.699
I'm curious, what did he tell you?

00:06:48.699 --> 00:06:50.520
Did he make any threats?

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:55.919
And a quick follow up, if I may, sir, the
list of your political opponents who are dead,

00:06:55.919 --> 00:06:58.779
prison or jail, is long.

00:06:58.779 --> 00:07:04.220
Alexei Navalny, this organization called for
free and fair elections an end to corruption.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:07.870
But Russia has outlawed that organization,
calling it extremists.

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:11.300
And you have now prevented anyone who supports
him to run for office.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:14.690
So my question is, Mr. President, what are
you so afraid of?

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:19.319
Speaker 3: It sure us, right?

00:07:19.319 --> 00:07:31.409
Well, once again, I would like to repeat what
I said about so-called foreign agents and

00:07:31.409 --> 00:07:42.879
the people who say that they are part of the
unauthorized opposition.

00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:52.270
I've already spoken to your colleagues, but
now I have to repeat that to you.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:08:03.509
OK, once again, the US has passed a law which
said that the US would particularly favor

00:08:03.509 --> 00:08:05.340
individual organizations in Russia.

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:07.389
Speaker 1: So it just goes nowhere.

00:08:07.389 --> 00:08:13.050
And the reporter points out you didn't answer
the question, what about those dead political

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:15.029
opponents in Russia?

00:08:15.029 --> 00:08:17.649
And Putin still will not answer.

00:08:17.649 --> 00:08:21.699
Speaker 4: You didn't answer my question,
sir.

00:08:21.699 --> 00:08:28.089
If all of your political opponents are dead
in prison poison, doesn't that send a message

00:08:28.089 --> 00:08:33.800
that you do not want a fair political fight
just yet?

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:46.460
Speaker 3: Well, on the question of who is
murdering whom, people rioted and went into

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:51.500
the Congress in the US with political demands.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:58.600
And many people were dictators, criminals,
and they are threatened with imprisonment

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:08.100
from 20 to 25 years, presumably the latest
on the subject.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:10.590
And these people were immediately arrested
after those events.

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:16.790
Speaker 1: He's called out and he just brings
up the January 6th Trump riots.

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:22.600
And again, for people who say, David, you're
too aggressive about Putin, I'm praising Putin.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.530
You've really got to hand it to the guy.

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:29.060
He doesn't get fazed by being called out for
the political murders that he's suspected

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:32.870
of being involved in or having ordered last
one.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:40.080
Putin asked about his relationship with the
West and how Russian politics is seen as unstable.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:42.210
Putin answers about everything other than
that.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:48.080
Take a look at this
Speaker 3: ABC News poll that Joe Biden presides

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:49.540
over stability.

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:57.550
But it's Kazuma of machineA is urging stable,
stable, predictable relations.

00:09:57.550 --> 00:10:07.970
But the West thinks that unpredictability
is a hallmark of Russian politics.

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:13.280
How do you think you can improve relations
with the West?

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:21.590
Well, I think you've really got to a high
level up there in view, the first part of

00:10:21.590 --> 00:10:29.360
your question that the West considers, are
you going to reject that?

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:36.210
You say, allow me, first of all, to answer
the first part of your question.

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:41.310
You said that in Russia, Russian foreign policy
is unpredictable.

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:47.530
Allow me to respond, first of all, with a
couple of facts.

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:53.690
What about the withdrawal of the U.S. from
the ABM Treaty?

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:54.690
Absolutely unpredictable.

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:55.890
Speaker 1: That was.

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:59.680
So I'm going to stop it there because it it
just goes nowhere.

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:03.160
Putin has this ability to talk and talk and
talk and say nothing.

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:05.290
That is really stunning.

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:09.280
And it it I wonder, does he do this with Biden?

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:11.930
In other words, during the summit, did the
summit end early?

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:12.930
Maybe.

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:14.150
We don't know why it ended early.

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:17.570
I think a lot of it was sort of like, what
else are we going to talk about?

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:20.270
Did the summit end early because Putin was
doing this stuff?

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:27.820
It's just an incredible filibustering and
so really unsurprising, but amazing how Putin

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:29.840
is just able to do this and do this and do
this.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:33.880
And as a reminder, I don't want war with Russia.

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:40.490
I don't see Russia as a unique enemy, but
I don't play coy with authoritarian types

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:42.670
and I find nothing progressive in it.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:49.550
And me, as a progressive social Democrat,
I find things to criticize about the United

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:50.630
States.

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:55.490
I'm against American imperialism around the
world, on and on and on.

00:11:55.490 --> 00:12:00.930
And also I see very little that is redeeming
about governments that function the way that

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:02.530
Putin's Russia functions.

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:03.900
But I don't want war with Russia.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:11.260
Guys don't email me saying I'm suggesting
or implying that we need to attack Russia

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:13.110
because I'm not.

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:16.660
And if you believe that, I would challenge
you to find even one instance of me saying

00:12:16.660 --> 00:13:14.410
so.

